CC   On or before Apr 4, 2002 this sequence version replaced gi:208963,
CC   gi:58257.
CC   The circular sequence is numbered such that 0 is the middle of the
CC   unique EcoRI site and the count increases first through the tet
CC   genes, the pMB1 material, and finally through the Tn3 region.
CC   Plasmid pBR322 contains ampicillin and tetracycline resistance
CC   genes.  The ampicillin resistance gene (amp-r) is a penicillin
CC   beta-lactamase. Promoters P1 and P3 are for the beta-lactamase
CC   gene. P3 is the natural promoter, and P1 is artificially created by
CC   the ligation of two different DNA fragments to create pBR322. P2 is
CC   in the same region as P1, but it is on the opposite strand and
CC   initiates transcription in the direction of the tetracycline
CC   resistance gene.
CC   Mutational studies in the primosome assembly sites indicate four
CC   types of mutations:  Class I having no effect on the activities
CC   elicited by the DNA site and the bases involved are probably
CC   spacers; Class II requiring higher Mg-2+ concentrations than the
CC   wild-type to be fully activated as factor Y ATPase effectors; Class
CC   III co-inactivating both the ATPase effector and DNA replication
CC   template activity of the site, indicating that they probably
CC   represent essential contact points between factor Y and the DNA;
CC   Class IV having a replication template activity intermediate that
CC   of class III and class II mutant DNAs.
CC   Specific sites within or near the origins of replication are
CC   recognized by dnaA protein.  Without dnaA binding to the origin of
CC   replication chromosomal replication is not possible (15). pBR322
CC   DNA contains two separate regions on opposite strands and close to
CC   the origin of replication which, when in single-stranded form, can
CC   act as effectors for the ATPase activity of E.coli replication
CC   factor Y (5).  Small fragments of DNA containing these sites when
CC   cloned in an f1 phage vector act as origins of DNA replication
CC   allowing the formation of complementary double-stranded DNA in
CC   rifampicin-resistant, dna(B,G,C)-dependent fashion in vitro (5).
CC   The biological activity of echinomycin is thought to be related to
CC   the formation of complexes by intercalating with cellular DNA (20).
CC   Complete source information:
CC   Plasmid pBR322 from E.coli (2),(1),(3),(6),(11),(8),(5),(7),(12),
CC   (13),(10),(9),(14),(18),(19),(15),(20),(16); pBR322 DNA in pXf3
CC   (4).
CC   The following data and their annotation were supplied by Will
CC   Gilbert under the auspices of the Curator Program.
